    <article>
      <header class="article-header">
        <div class="section-tag">Accountability | Sagamok</div>
        <h1>Inside Sagamok's GR Truss deal</h1>
        <p class="deck">A $100 purchase, $900,000 in Council approvals and a documented link to the company behind the $7 million South Market sale.</p>
        <p class="byline"><strong>By Laura Owl</strong><br>July 25, 2026 | Investigative draft for RHT Circle</p>
      </header>

      <figure class="hero">
        <img src="/images/review/gr-truss-investigation-2026-07-25/gr-truss-story-2-of-3.png" alt="Graphic showing the passed March 2026 repayment deadline for Sagamok's 500,000 dollar GR Truss advance.">
        <figcaption>Motion 2025/04 #05 approved $500,000 at 4 per cent, repayable by March 2026. The available minutes do not say whether it was repaid.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="article-grid">
        <div class="story">
          <p class="lead">Three records tell the beginning of Sagamok's GR Truss story. The audited statements show a $100 purchase. Council minutes show $900,000 in later approvals. An Ontario security filing connects GR Truss equipment to the same numbered company that sold Sagamok the South Market property for $7 million.</p>

          <p>The records establish those facts. They do not explain the commercial agreements behind them.</p>

          <p>The first unanswered question now has a passed deadline. Council approved a $500,000 cash-flow advance at 4 per cent on April 2, 2025. The money was supposed to be repaid by March 2026.</p>

          <p>That date has passed. A review of every Council minute currently held by RHT Circle found the original repayment term, but no later entry recording that the $500,000 and interest were paid back. The minutes also do not record an extension or say what balance remains.</p>

          <p>The latest meeting in the held archive is May 19, 2026. Four meetings after the end of March are available: April 7, April 18, May 12 and May 19. None reports repayment of the original advance.</p>

          <p>Instead, on May 12, Council approved another $150,000 for GR Truss's immediate cash-flow needs. That separate advance was to be repaid within six months.</p>

          <blockquote>
            “Sagamok Chief &amp; Council motion to support the administration advancing funds to GR Truss for $500,000.00 to support the cashflow needs, repayable at 4% by March 2026.”
            <cite>Council minutes, April 2, 2025, Motion 2025/04 #05</cite>
          </blockquote>

          <p>The missing minute entry does not prove the money was not repaid. A payment could have occurred without appearing in the posted minutes. It does mean members cannot determine the answer from the Council record they have been given.</p>

          <div class="plain-box">
            <strong>The question is narrow</strong>
            <p>Was the $500,000 plus 4 per cent paid back? A repayment ledger, bank record, interest calculation or written extension would settle it.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>A $100 purchase followed by $900,000 in approvals</h2>

          <p>Sagamok's audited financial statements say the Nation acquired 100 per cent of GR Truss on April 1, 2025 for $100. The purchase ran through Sagamok's real-estate ownership structure.</p>

          <p>A $100 share price does not mean the business had only $100 in value, assets or debt. In a share purchase, the buyer takes control of the company as it stands, including its assets and liabilities. The closing balance sheet and debt schedule are therefore central records.</p>

          <p>After the acquisition, Council approved four amounts connected to GR Truss:</p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>$500,000</strong> on April 2, 2025 for cash flow, at 4 per cent, repayable by March 2026.</li>
            <li><strong>$125,000</strong> on September 23, 2025 for lot development in Carling Township.</li>
            <li><strong>$125,000</strong> again on January 22, 2026, using the same stated purpose.</li>
            <li><strong>$150,000</strong> on May 12, 2026 for immediate cash flow, repayable within six months.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>The four approvals total $900,000. Approval does not by itself prove every dollar was paid or spent. The minutes do not provide the disbursement records, current company statements or a reconciliation of the two same-wording $125,000 contributions.</p>

          <h2>The board dates do not line up in the posted record</h2>

          <p>The Ontario corporate profile names three people as the complete GR Truss director and officer slate: Chief Angus Toulouse as president, Councillor Michael Abitong as secretary and Councillor Norma Petahtegoose as treasurer.</p>

          <p>The registry records all six director and officer roles as beginning April 1, 2025, the acquisition date.</p>

          <p>But the April 2 Council minutes say a board still “will need to be appointed” and that selected members would be brought before Council. Council's posted appointment motion did not come until May 27, 56 days after the registry start dates.</p>

          <p>That sequence does not prove the appointments were invalid. A shareholder resolution, closing resolution or other corporate authority could have made the appointments effective April 1. That document is not in the held record.</p>

          <figure>
            <img src="/images/review/gr-truss-investigation-2026-07-25/gr-truss-story-1-of-3.png" alt="Graphic comparing the April 1 Ontario registry start dates with Council's May 27 GR Truss appointment.">
            <figcaption>The registry records the three directors and officers beginning April 1. Council's posted appointment is dated May 27.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>The land seller appears in the GR Truss security record</h2>

          <p>A separate certified filing connects GR Truss to another major Sagamok transaction.</p>

          <p>On November 13, 2024, 1000602533 Ontario Inc. transferred the South Market property in Sault Ste. Marie to Sagamok's real-estate company for $7 million.</p>

          <p>Two months later, an Ontario Personal Property Security Registration listed that same numbered company and McLeod Consulting together as secured parties over a John Deere payloader belonging to GR Truss. The filing was registered January 14, 2025, about eleven weeks before Sagamok acquired the truss company.</p>

          <p>The filing proves the two companies shared a registered security position over that equipment. It does not state the amount, the underlying agreement, who paid for the machine or why the security was shared. It also does not show that Sagamok created the arrangement. The registration predates Sagamok's takeover.</p>

          <p>The acquisition closing schedule should show whether the security was disclosed and what debts or liens Sagamok assumed. The underlying security agreement should explain the commercial reason.</p>

          <figure>
            <img src="/images/review/gr-truss-investigation-2026-07-25/gr-truss-story-3-of-3.png" alt="Graphic showing the South Market seller and McLeod Consulting together in a pre-acquisition GR Truss security registration.">
            <figcaption>The same numbered company appears in the $7 million South Market transfer and in a GR Truss equipment-security filing.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>What the audit does and does not answer</h2>

          <p>Sagamok's latest audited financial statements received a clean, unmodified opinion from BDO Canada LLP. Nothing in the audit establishes wrongdoing by GR Truss, Council or any person named in the records.</p>

          <p>The audit also cannot answer whether the $500,000 was repaid by March 2026. The financial year ended March 31, 2025, before the advance was approved and before Sagamok's April 1 acquisition took effect.</p>

          <p>The repayment answer belongs in later accounting records: the GR Truss ledger, Sagamok's bank records, the interest calculation, any written extension and the company's current stand-alone financial statements.</p>

          <div class="questions">
            <h2>Five records would settle the story</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>The repayment ledger and bank record for the $500,000 advance.</li>
              <li>The 4 per cent interest calculation and any written extension.</li>
              <li>The GR Truss share-purchase agreement and closing balance sheet.</li>
              <li>The agreement behind PPSR file 512630307 and its current balance.</li>
              <li>Current GR Truss financial statements and board minutes.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <p>Members do not need a theory about what happened. They need the records showing whether the money came back.</p>
        </div>

